/*
==============================================================================
  PoC 02 -- full exploitation chain: a cleartext key proved secret
==============================================================================

  CLASS      : missed attack (false "verified")
  SEVERITY   : critical -- end-to-end wrong security verdict, fully green report
  AFFECTS    : tamarin-prover 1.12.0 and develop @ ef3f0468 (1.13.0)
  STATUS     : same root cause as PoC 01.  Confirmed by the maintainers; fix
               proposed in PR #915 (open).  Soundness label disputed; see
               PoC 01 and README.md.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THIS ADDS OVER PoC 01
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  PoC 01 needs an explicit [use_induction] annotation, which a reviewer might
  notice.  This one does not.

  Two facts combine into a whole-theory compromise:

    1. `[sources]` implies UseInduction with no annotation from the user
       (lib/theory/src/ClosedTheory.hs:120):

           inductionHint
             | any (`elem` [SourceLemma, InvariantLemma]) (get lAttributes l)
                 = UseInduction

    2. `[sources]` lemmas are consumed by `refineWithSourceAsms` at
       theory-close time (lib/theory/src/CloseRule.hs:426), i.e. BEFORE any
       proof runs, and the refined sources are then used by EVERY lemma in the
       theory.

  So a single misclassified restriction (PoC 01) silently "proves" a false
  sources lemma, which in turn prunes legitimate sources out of the
  precomputation for the entire file.

  Note what makes this a defect rather than the documented behaviour of
  [sources] lemmas.  A [sources] lemma is an assumption, and a theory whose
  sources lemma is reported FALSIFIED gives the user that signal: everything
  downstream is conditional on something visibly unmet.  Here the sources
  lemma is WRONGLY PROVED, because of the classifier defect in PoC 01, so the
  precondition looks met and no signal is emitted at all.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  GROUND TRUTH
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `Gen` outputs the fresh key k directly on the public channel via Out(k).
  The adversary trivially learns it.  `secrecy` is FALSE.
  `bogus_sources` is FALSE.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  TAMARIN SAYS (1.12.0, unpatched)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    bogus_sources (all-traces):   verified (3 steps)   <-- WRONG
    secrecy       (all-traces):   verified (2 steps)   <-- WRONG
    gen_reachable (exists-trace): verified (2 steps)
    /* All wellformedness checks were successful. */

  In the proof output, `secrecy` closes with `by solve( !KU( ~n ) @ #j )`:
  the false sources refinement removed the only case that mattered.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHY THIS IS THE INTERESTING RESULT
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every hygiene signal a Tamarin user is taught to rely on comes back green:

    * wellformedness report is clean
    * no `sorry` anywhere in the proof
    * the `exists-trace` liveness witness confirms the model is NOT vacuous
    * no lemma is reported falsified, so nothing looks suspicious in the
      summary

  There is no signal available to the user, at any verbosity, that the
  verdict is wrong.

  Deleting only the sources lemma restores `secrecy: falsified`, which
  confirms the mechanism.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOTE ON THE RESTRICTION -- AND SCOPE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `GenIsNotLast` is written the way a modeller would express "the protocol
  does not stop right after key generation".  The manual's machine-tested
  grammar accepts `last()` in the body of a restriction; see the DISPUTE
  section of PoC 01.

  HOWEVER -- and this bounds the practical impact, so do not overstate it:

    NO SHIPPED CASE STUDY IS AFFECTED.

  All 1042 .spthy files under examples/ were scanned.  36 contain the string
  "last(".  In every one of them the occurrence is inside Tamarin's OWN
  machine-generated proof text (lines such as
  `solve( (last(#i)) | (Ex #j. Update(k,r) @ #j & ...) )`, emitted by the
  induction rule and saved into the analyzed output), never in a user-written
  restriction.  Zero case studies put `last()` in a restriction, which is what
  this bug requires.

  So the accurate claim is: a live, reachable unsoundness in syntax the
  grammar accepts, which currently affects no published Tamarin result.  It is
  a trap waiting for a user, not evidence that existing verification results
  are wrong.  Anyone citing this should say so in those words.
  (Scanner: tools/lastscan.py.)

  See 01_last_induction_unsound.spthy for the root cause analysis, the control
  experiment that isolates the mechanism, and the soundness-label dispute.
==============================================================================
*/

theory LastSourcesChain begin

rule Gen:  [ Fr(k) ] --[ Gen(k)  ]-> [ Out(k) ]
rule Recv: [ In(x)  ] --[ Recv(x) ]-> [        ]

// Misclassified as "// safety formula" by isSafetyFormula.
restriction GenIsNotLast:
  "All k #i. Gen(k) @ i ==> not(last(#i))"

// FALSE. [sources] implies use_induction, so the unsound induction proves it,
// and it is fed into source refinement for every lemma below.
lemma bogus_sources [sources]:
  "All k #i. Gen(k) @ i ==> F"

// FALSE: k is sent in the clear by rule Gen.
lemma secrecy:
  "All k #i #j. Gen(k) @ i & KU(k) @ j ==> F"

// Sanity check: the model is not degenerate.
lemma gen_reachable: exists-trace
  "Ex k #i. Gen(k) @ i"

end
